Overview

This lab builds a small fully connected Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) from scratch with NumPy to understand how information moves forward through a network and how the error signal moves backward to update its parameters.

Architecture: 2 inputs -> 2 hidden units -> 1 output (sigmoid)

The model takes a 2D feature vector (not a raw image) and is used to learn the dense-layer and gradient-flow concepts that appear inside larger deep learning systems.

What is covered
Forward propagation with ReLU, tanh and sigmoid activations
Binary cross-entropy (BCE) loss
Backpropagation using the chain rule (sigmoid + BCE gives dL/dz2 = y - t)
One gradient-descent update and a new forward pass to confirm the loss decreases
Gradient verification using an independent implementation (finite differences in NumPy, and PyTorch autograd)
Experiments
Replace ReLU with tanh and compare hidden activations and output probability
Change the target from t = 1 to t = 0 and recompute loss and gradients
Compare learning rates 0.01, 0.1 and 0.5
Use one positive and one negative pre-activation to see the ReLU derivative mask
Compare manual gradients with PyTorch autograd (maximum absolute difference)

An observation table summarising the results is included at the end of the notebook.

Key results (ReLU, t = 1, lr = 0.1)
Quantity	Value
Hidden pre-activation z1	[1.5, -4.0]
Hidden output h	[1.5, 0.0]
Output probability y	0.880797
BCE loss	0.126928
Loss after one update	0.093081
Max gradient difference (finite differences)	~8.6e-11
